1
00:00:00,460 --> 00:00:05,020
Health Dividend Six thousand six hundred and fifty diseases have zero approved treatments.

2
00:00:05,560 --> 00:00:09,660
At your current pace, you'll get to all of them in four hundred and forty-three years.

3
00:00:10,300 --> 00:00:17,880
Redirect one percent of military spending and it drops to thirty-six years, preventing ten point seven billion deaths at

4
00:00:18,100 --> 00:00:23,520
fifty thousand three hundred times the cost-effectiveness of anti-malaria bed nets.

5
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:27,700
The whole thing costs less per year to run than Halloween costumes for dogs.

6
00:00:28,280 --> 00:00:34,620
But sure, take your time. That was the peace dividend: the money you get from buying one percent fewer explosions.

7
00:00:35,200 --> 00:00:39,700
Now here is the health dividend: what happens when you spend it on medicine instead.

8
00:00:40,620 --> 00:00:47,440
Right now, somewhere in your regulatory system, a drug that already passed safety testing is sitting in a filing cabinet

9
00:00:47,440 --> 00:00:55,020
while the disease it treats keeps killing people. Somewhere else, a researcher with a promising compound for one of six

10
00:00:55,020 --> 00:01:00,940
thousand six hundred and fifty untreated diseases is filling out the same form for the fourth time.

11
00:01:01,880 --> 00:01:04,720
The health dividend is what happens when you stop doing this.

12
00:01:06,140 --> 00:01:12,120
It is worth eighty-four point eight quadrillion dollars. That number is not a typo.

13
00:01:12,700 --> 00:01:17,180
I checked. Your economists checked. Everyone checked. It's still that number.

14
00:01:17,800 --> 00:01:22,980
The rest of this chapter is the receipt. From Explosions to Trial Slots.

15
00:01:24,220 --> 00:01:28,870
twenty-seven point two billion dollars arrives from the peace dividend.

16
00:01:29,120 --> 00:01:36,080
Investor and political allocations take their cut, leaving twenty-one point eight billion dollars for medical research (eighty

17
00:01:36,080 --> 00:01:41,780
percent of the total). Your decentralized F.D.A. takes forty million dollars to keep the lights on.

18
00:01:42,160 --> 00:01:46,220
That leaves twenty-one point seven billion dollars for trial subsidies.

19
00:01:47,080 --> 00:01:52,490
I am now going to show you what that money buys. I apologize in advance for the number of numbers.

20
00:01:52,860 --> 00:01:58,960
Your species requires receipts before it will believe anything, even things that are obviously true.

21
00:01:59,820 --> 00:02:06,740
Especially things that are obviously true. Your current system charges forty-one thousand dollars per patient in a clinical

22
00:02:06,740 --> 00:02:15,020
trial. I asked why and was told "regulatory compliance, site management, and monitoring overhead," which I believe is the

23
00:02:15,140 --> 00:02:22,080
human phrase for "permission costs more than medicine." Pragmatic trials, the kind your decentralized F.D.A.

24
00:02:22,220 --> 00:02:29,900
runs, cost nine hundred and twenty-nine dollars. At that price, twenty-one point seven billion dollars buys you twenty-three

25
00:02:29,900 --> 00:02:38,120
point four million patient trial slots per year. Your species currently enrolls one point nine million patients per year

26
00:02:38,120 --> 00:02:45,210
in clinical trials globally. The treaty adds twelve point three times more capacity on top of that.

27
00:02:45,300 --> 00:02:48,260
Remember this number. Everything that follows depends on it.

28
00:02:49,200 --> 00:02:57,360
The Waiting List. You met the queue in the untapped therapeutic frontier: six thousand six hundred and fifty diseases with

29
00:02:57,360 --> 00:03:02,180
zero approved treatments, and fifteen getting their first treatment per year.

30
00:03:02,880 --> 00:03:06,880
At that rate, you clear the backlog in four hundred and forty-three years.

31
00:03:07,540 --> 00:03:12,440
I will let you sit with that number for a moment. It is longer than your species has had writing.

32
00:03:13,220 --> 00:03:20,000
For context, four hundred and forty-three years ago, Shakespeare was writing plays, Galileo was building telescopes, and

33
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:25,340
your species already had printing presses, universities, and intercontinental shipping.

34
00:03:25,340 --> 00:03:32,520
You just had not gotten around to curing most diseases. At this pace, you still will not for another four hundred and forty-three

35
00:03:32,520 --> 00:03:39,390
years. Apply the twelve point three times multiplier. Instead of fifteen diseases getting their first treatment per year,

36
00:03:39,440 --> 00:03:45,680
you get one hundred and eighty-five diseases per year. The queue clears in thirty-six years instead of four hundred and

37
00:03:45,680 --> 00:03:51,940
forty-three years. The average untreated disease gets its first treatment two hundred and four years earlier.

38
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:57,680
That is the difference between "your grandchildren might see a cure" and "you might see a cure."

39
00:03:58,460 --> 00:04:06,080
On Wishonia, we consider this a meaningful distinction. On Earth, it apparently requires a seventy-eight-page chapter to

40
00:04:06,080 --> 00:04:13,460
justify. Your species figured out how to split atoms in six years during a war, but letting a cancer patient take a pill

41
00:04:13,460 --> 00:04:18,920
that passed safety testing requires the gestation period of three consecutive elephants.

42
00:04:19,600 --> 00:04:22,900
The twelve point three times multiplier compresses the elephant parade.

43
00:04:23,940 --> 00:04:31,200
But trial capacity is only half the delay. Once a drug passes safety testing, your regulatory system sits on it for another

44
00:04:31,200 --> 00:04:37,330
eight point two years to confirm it works well enough, while people who could be taking it die instead.

45
00:04:38,040 --> 00:04:45,660
Add the capacity acceleration and the efficacy lag together: two hundred and twelve years of dying, condensed into not dying.

46
00:04:47,120 --> 00:04:53,520
The Body Count. One hundred and fifty thousand deaths per day die from disease every day.

47
00:04:54,180 --> 00:04:57,960
While you were reading the previous section, approximately three hundred of them did.

48
00:04:58,540 --> 00:05:01,370
They are not coming back to find out how the math turned out.

49
00:05:01,760 --> 00:05:08,040
Not all of those deaths are currently preventable, but ninety-two point six percent are eventually avoidable with sufficient

50
00:05:08,080 --> 00:05:12,680
biomedical research. "Eventually avoidable" does not mean "currently preventable."

51
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:17,080
It means: given enough research, these diseases have biological solutions.

52
00:05:17,820 --> 00:05:22,850
The only category excluded is accidents (falling off things, being hit by things).

53
00:05:23,140 --> 00:05:30,140
Every disease, including aging, is a biological process, and biological processes are, in principle, fixable.

54
00:05:30,900 --> 00:05:38,540
This is the theoretical ceiling, not a near-term promise. Over a two hundred and twelve-year timeline shift, the cumulative

55
00:05:38,540 --> 00:05:48,560
toll: ten point seven billion deaths. That is five hundred and sixty-five billion disability-adjusted life years, or D.A.L.Y.s,

56
00:05:48,800 --> 00:05:54,140
of healthy life, and one point nine three quadrillion hours of suffering eliminated.

57
00:05:55,060 --> 00:06:02,080
These are cumulative, one-time benefits from permanently accelerating the discovery pipeline, the same methodology used

58
00:06:02,080 --> 00:06:07,470
to value smallpox eradication. Nobody objected to the methodology when it was used for smallpox.

59
00:06:08,260 --> 00:06:14,240
They will object now because the number is larger, which is a strange reason to object to saving more people.

60
00:06:15,460 --> 00:06:23,120
The Price Tag on Not Being Dead. Your governments already put a price on human life when deciding whether to approve drugs,

61
00:06:23,480 --> 00:06:30,360
build highways, or regulate pollution. The standard rate: one hundred and fifty thousand dollars per quality-adjusted life

62
00:06:30,360 --> 00:06:37,630
year. Your species finds this uncomfortable to discuss but does it constantly.

63
00:06:38,240 --> 00:06:41,620
You just prefer to do it in footnotes, where the dying can't see.

64
00:06:42,500 --> 00:06:47,520
The world loses two point eight eight billion D.A.L.Y.s per year.

65
00:06:48,120 --> 00:06:51,960
Ninety-two point six percent are eventually avoidable with sufficient research.

66
00:06:52,620 --> 00:07:00,700
At one hundred and fifty thousand dollars per quality-adjusted life year, or Q.A.L.Y., that is four hundred trillion dollars

67
00:07:00,700 --> 00:07:08,180
per year in welfare losses. Multiply by the two hundred and twelve-year timeline shift and you get the total economic value

68
00:07:08,180 --> 00:07:12,960
of the health dividend: eighty-four point eight quadrillion dollars.

69
00:07:14,040 --> 00:07:22,880
"But that is more than global G.D.P.!" Yes. G.D.P. (one hundred and fifteen trillion dollars) measures market transactions.

70
00:07:23,580 --> 00:07:32,680
It does not measure the value of not being dead, not being in pain, and not watching your children die of treatable diseases.

71
00:07:33,740 --> 00:07:38,600
The one hundred and fifty thousand dollars per quality-adjusted life year, or Q.A.L.Y.

72
00:07:38,820 --> 00:07:46,020
figure is not something I invented. I just multiplied it by the number of sick people, which is also not my fault.

73
00:07:47,180 --> 00:07:53,420
The only debatable input is whether the timeline shift is really approximately two hundred and twelve years.

74
00:07:54,420 --> 00:08:00,740
See the full derivation for how that number is calculated, and the Monte Carlo simulations for what happens when every input

75
00:08:00,740 --> 00:08:08,880
varies. The Receipt. Your decentralized F.D.A. costs forty million dollars a year to operate.

76
00:08:09,780 --> 00:08:15,740
For reference, this is roughly what your species spends annually on Halloween costumes for dogs.

77
00:08:16,540 --> 00:08:24,860
Less than one fighter jet. Less than what FIFA spends on "hospitality" (your word for bribing officials in a building with

78
00:08:24,860 --> 00:08:34,100
chandeliers). A system that could test cures for seven thousand diseases costs less to run than the outfits you put on animals

79
00:08:34,100 --> 00:08:42,559
that do not want to wear them. The R and D savings alone are fifty-eight point six billion dollars a year, a six hundred

80
00:08:42,559 --> 00:08:47,380
and thirty-seven to one return without counting a single human life.

81
00:08:48,240 --> 00:08:54,540
Include the lives (which you would think someone would, but your species has a long history of not counting the people who

82
00:08:54,540 --> 00:09:03,000
die quietly), and the treaty's total societal return hits eighty-four point eight million to one.

83
00:09:04,020 --> 00:09:08,540
The cost per year of healthy life saved: eighty-four cents.

84
00:09:09,680 --> 00:09:16,840
Anti-malaria bed nets, the gold standard of cost-effective interventions, cost eighty-nine dollars per disability-adjusted

85
00:09:16,940 --> 00:09:25,880
life year, or D.A.L.Y. This system operates at fifty thousand three hundred times the cost-effectiveness, at vastly greater

86
00:09:25,880 --> 00:09:33,080
scale. This costs less per life-year than mosquito nets and nobody has heard of it.

87
00:09:33,620 --> 00:09:41,760
Your species has spent more money marketing energy drinks. The total campaign investment: one billion dollars.

88
00:09:42,340 --> 00:09:50,980
Even the worst case (everything goes wrong, adoption is terrible, the servers catch fire) still returns an estimated sixty-six

89
00:09:51,220 --> 00:09:57,180
to one return. Your worst medical outcome is better than your best military one.

90
00:09:58,180 --> 00:10:05,820
Every day without this system costs one hundred and sixty-one million dollars in wasted trial spending alone.

91
00:10:06,500 --> 00:10:12,480
The regulatory lag is not a safety feature. It is a waiting room where people die.

92
00:10:12,920 --> 00:10:21,120
You just gave it a nicer name. For you personally: if diseases get cured even a few years earlier, the average human gains

93
00:10:21,120 --> 00:10:28,820
roughly fifty thousand extra hours of life. You spent seventeen minutes reading this chapter.

94
00:10:29,380 --> 00:10:34,100
That is a one hundred and seventy-six thousand times return on your time.

95
00:10:34,600 --> 00:10:42,540
Your best investment ever was reading. I realize the irony, given that most of you stopped reading twelve paragraphs ago.

96
